<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/calculator.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/calculator-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/calculator.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="768" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Carrier Estimate Review</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Insurance Estimate Review in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Not every short estimate needs a full appraisal. Sometimes the fastest path is to find exactly what’s missing, put it in writing and ask the insurer to revisit its numbers.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Deliverable</small><b>Written findings</b></div><div><small>Before</small><b>Any appraisal demand</b></div><div><small>Useful for</small><b>Supplements and decisions</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Insurance estimate review in Wisconsin gives you an independent, line-by-line read of your insurer’s estimate before you decide what to do next. We compare the carrier’s scope and pricing against the actual damage and current local costs, then tell you in plain language where the gaps are and how large they appear to be.</p>
<h2>Why start with a review</h2>
<p>Appraisal is a formal process with costs on both sides. Before committing to it, it helps to know whether the difference between your contractor’s bid and the carrier’s estimate comes from a few missed items or a fundamentally different view of the loss. A review answers that question. Many claims move forward with a supplement request; others clearly call for appraisal. Either way, you decide with better information.</p>
<h2>What we check</h2>
<h3>Scope</h3>
<ul>
<li>Rooms, roof slopes or elevations missing from the estimate entirely</li>
<li>Measurements that don’t match the property</li>
<li>Tear-out, prep and protection steps that were skipped</li>
<li>Items repaired that should be replaced, or vice versa</li>
</ul>
<h3>Pricing</h3>
<ul>
<li>Unit prices compared with current local market rates</li>
<li>Labor minimums and specialty trades left out</li>
<li>Overhead and profit where multiple trades are involved</li>
</ul>
<h3>Policy application</h3>
<ul>
<li>Depreciation percentages and whether labor was depreciated</li>
<li>Code upgrade items and ordinance or law coverage</li>
<li>Deductibles and sublimits applied correctly</li>
</ul>
<h2>What you receive</h2>
<p>We provide written findings organized by category, with an estimate of the dollar difference where we can support one. If it helps, we can prepare a supplement-ready summary you can send to your adjuster. We don’t negotiate the claim on your behalf; we give you the information to do that, or to decide on appraisal.</p>
<p>Reviews work for every kind of property we see, from a single bathroom leak in a Kenosha ranch to a hail claim on a Fox Valley apartment complex. The scale changes, but the method doesn’t: compare what the estimate says against what the building actually needs, and show the difference clearly enough that an adjuster can act on it.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Illustrative example</b>Suppose a carrier estimates a kitchen water loss at $14,000 and your contractor bids $26,000. A review might show that $7,000 of the gap comes from cabinets priced as repairable and missing flooring continuity into the adjoining room, while the rest is a pricing difference. Those figures are illustrative only, but the breakdown tells you where to push.</div>
<h2>When a review leads to appraisal</h2>
<p>If the insurer won’t revisit its estimate after a supplement, or the gap is mostly about judgment calls the adjuster won’t change, appraisal may be the next step. Our review becomes the foundation for that work, so nothing is wasted. Learn more about <a href="/appraisal-solutions/appraisal-demand-representation/">appraisal demand representation</a> or read the <a href="/appraisal-process-guide/">appraisal process guide</a>.</p>
<h2>Tools to use first</h2>
<p>Our free <a href="/insurance-claim-calculators/">claim calculators</a> can give you a rough sense of depreciation and the size of your gap. The <a href="/claims-dictionary/">claims dictionary</a> explains terms you’ll see on an estimate, and the <a href="/insurance-appraisal-faq/">appraisal FAQ</a> covers common questions. When you’re ready, <a href="/contact-us/">send us the estimate</a>.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>What do I need to send for an estimate review?</summary><div class="a"><p>The insurer’s estimate, any contractor bids, photos of the damage and your policy’s declarations page. The full policy helps if depreciation or code coverage is in question.</p></div></details><details><summary>Will the insurer accept your review findings?</summary><div class="a"><p>There’s no guarantee. Many adjusters will reconsider specific, documented items, and if they won’t, the review gives you a solid basis for appraisal.</p></div></details><details><summary>Is an estimate review the same as hiring a public adjuster?</summary><div class="a"><p>No. We review and report on the estimate. We don’t take over or negotiate your claim.</p></div></details></div>
